// Adversarial stress: Siphon protocol under sustained chaos input — Dense layout.
//
// Siphon's 3-system pipeline: siphon_tick_streak (decrements window_remaining),
// siphon_on_cell_destroyed (consumes Destroyed<Cell>, updates SiphonStreak, emits
// ReverseTimePenalty on non-first kills), and siphon_cleanup_node (resets streak on
// node exit). This stress scenario targets four failure paths:
//
//   1. siphon_tick_streak / siphon_on_cell_destroyed race: siphon_tick_streak runs
//      before siphon_on_cell_destroyed in the same FixedUpdate tick. If both systems
//      observe a streak at its exact expiry threshold (window_remaining drains to <= 0
//      in tick N; siphon_on_cell_destroyed sees kill_count == 0 in the same tick), the
//      very next kill is incorrectly treated as a streak-opener rather than a streak
//      continuation. Dense layout maximises kill density, increasing the chance that
//      a kill lands in the exact tick window_remaining crosses zero. NoNaN catches
//      a NaN streak value if the subtraction underflows; RunStatsMonotonic is not
//      listed (DebtCollector uses it) but BoltSpeedAccurate catches downstream speed
//      corruption if ReverseTimePenalty fires with a malformed value.
//
//   2. Destroyed<Cell> reader accumulation on rapid kills: Dense layout drives rapid
//      cell destruction, filling the Destroyed<Cell> message queue in a single tick.
//      siphon_on_cell_destroyed iterates all messages per tick; if the reader is not
//      drained correctly (e.g., a missed reader.clear() branch), messages accumulate
//      and each subsequent tick replays them, applying ReverseTimePenalty multiple
//      times. NoEntityLeaks catches unconsumed message entities; TimerNonNegative
//      catches a node timer driven negative by repeated reverse-time applications.
//
//   3. siphon_cleanup_node cross-node isolation: allow_early_end false triggers node
//      restarts. cleanup_node must fire on OnExit(NodeState::Playing) and reset
//      SiphonStreak to default. If cleanup races with a Destroyed<Cell> message buffered
//      from the final tick of the dying node, the new node begins with kill_count > 0
//      and window_remaining > 0, causing the first kill of the new node to award time
//      (via the continuation path) instead of starting a new streak silently. This is
//      impossible to detect directly, but NoNaN and BoltSpeedAccurate catch downstream
//      corruption if the awarded time drives the timer into an invalid state.
//
//   4. ReverseTimePenalty boundary: the penalty emitted by siphon_on_cell_destroyed
//      is applied by NodeSystems::ApplyTimePenalty. Dense layout with chaos input
//      can drive hundreds of ReverseTimePenalty events per run, each adding seconds
//      back to the node timer. TimerMonotonicallyDecreasing is not listed (it would
//      fire legitimately when Siphon is active), but BreakerInBounds and NoNaN
//      guard that the timer value stays representable.
//
// 3000 frames with allow_early_end: false sustains pressure across node restarts.
// 16 runs explore diverse Dense cell arrangements and kill-timing distributions.
(
    breaker: "Aegis",
    layout: "Dense",
    input: Chaos((action_prob: 0.7)),
    max_frames: 3000,
    disallowed_failures: [
        NoNaN,
        BoltInBounds,
        BreakerInBounds,
        BoltSpeedAccurate,
        ValidDashState,
        BreakerCountReasonable,
        NoEntityLeaks,
        BoltCountReasonable,
        SiphonStreakOrphaned,
    ],
    allowed_failures: None,
    debug_setup: None,
    allow_early_end: false,
    stress: (runs: 16, parallelism: 16),
    seed: 7712,
    frame_mutations: [
        (frame: 10, mutation: InjectProtocol(kind_name: "Siphon")),
    ],
)
